From: =?UTF-8?q?=C3=81ngel=20Ren=C3=A9?=
 <17342133+angelrenefm@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 13:51:15 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: exynos: google: gs201-panther: Give the MFC its
 memory pool
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Give panther's MFC its own reusable shared-dma-pool of 256 MiB below
4 GiB, and leave the node disabled. The driver takes its 64 MiB for the
firmware, contexts and codec buffers from the pool, firmware first, and
vb2 serves the OUTPUT and CAPTURE buffers from the rest. The firmware
rejects any buffer below its own base, which a single pool rules out.
256 MiB is what a 4K stream needs: a 3840x2160 frame in single-buffer
NV12 is 12.4 MiB in one block, and with 128 MiB next to the firmware
GStreamer got no frame at all out of a 4K clip. The pool is reusable, so
its pages serve movable allocations while nothing is decoded.

The node stays disabled until its two System MMUs are described: in
bypass, Google's closed firmware parsing untrusted video can reach all
memory below 4 GiB. An overlay in omarchy-mobile (device/panther)
enables it for owners who want hardware video.

Tested on a Pixel 7 booting a DTB with this node and pool enabled, the
driver built from this series as a module and the firmware taken from
the phone's own OTA. The pool is reserved below 4 GiB and the driver
probes from it on its own. H.264, HEVC, VP8 and VP9 decode bit-exact
against FFmpeg's and GStreamer's software decoders (1080p and 4K), and
Chromium plays 1080p and 4K H.264 and VP9 on the MFC.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Ángel René <17342133+angelrenefm@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 .../boot/dts/exynos/google/gs201-panther.dts  | 28 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 28 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/google/gs201-panther.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/google/gs201-panther.dts
index 25594305a..9986f4a90 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/google/gs201-panther.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/google/gs201-panther.dts
@@ -125,6 +125,24 @@ cont_splash_mem: splash@fac00000 {
 			reg = <0x0 0xfac00000 (1080 * 2400 * 4)>;
 			no-map;
 		};
+
+		/*
+		 * MFC firmware, contexts and frames. The MFC takes 32-bit
+		 * addresses, its System MMU is in bypass and the firmware
+		 * rejects any buffer below its own base, so they all come from
+		 * one contiguous pool below 4 GiB, firmware first. Reusable,
+		 * so the pages serve movable allocations while no video is
+		 * decoded. 256 MiB holds the firmware and a 4K stream decoded
+		 * to single-buffer NV12.
+		 */
+		mfc_pool: mfc-pool {
+			compatible = "shared-dma-pool";
+			reusable;
+			size = <0x10000000>;
+			/* read with the root's #address-cells (2), not #size-cells */
+			alignment = <0x0 0x200000>;
+			alloc-ranges = <0x0 0x80000000 0x80000000>;
+		};
 	};
 };
 
@@ -246,6 +264,16 @@ &gpu {
 	status = "okay";
 };
 
+/*
+ * Left disabled until its two System MMUs are described: in bypass, the
+ * closed firmware parsing untrusted video can reach all memory below
+ * 4 GiB. omarchy-mobile's device/panther overlay enables it for owners who
+ * want hardware video.
+ */
+&mfc {
+	memory-region = <&mfc_pool>;
+};
+
 &usbdrd31_phy {
 	status = "okay";
 };
